A||||9||johnmac||Flushing Water - Best Method||||1||
Z||000000||johnmac||10-11-2001||09:02 AM||||I getting ready to flush out my rebuilt V6 engine cooling system. What is the best procedure for flushing?<P>Someone told me to add ammonia to the water and let it set overnight.<P>Any suggestions? <P>------------------<BR>John MacHaffie<BR>86GT "Yellowbird"<BR>12+ month project car<BR>johnmac@mpinet.net<BR>Winter Springs, Florida||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fallman||10-11-2001||12:38 PM||||i was wondering about this as well??<P>my engine had an oil leak before rebuild and the coolant system has oil all over in it now. I flushed it by just running water though one hose and out the other when i replaced the radiator. But i seem to still have oil residue in the system its really cloudy, white and oily.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cozmo||10-11-2001||01:51 PM||||Fiero cooling systems are as much an art as a science... <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I used the Prestone backflush kit (Wallyworld &lt;$5) and their reccomended flush.<BR>I also removed the drain plugs on the coolant tubes in front of the rear wheels when I did it... a PITA but you should have seen the crud that came out!<BR>The crossflow radiator in the Fiero is an inefficient design and is prone to plugging up in the bottom rows. Best bet is tanking and rodding it (if it's not trashed) when you are working on the cooling system.  Any good radiator shop should know exactly what you want.<BR>Be wary of the V-6 water pump, some had plastic impellers that when worn would slip.<BR>When you fill it up, use the right mixtures of antifreeze for your climate and <B>definitely</B> read Ogre's fill and burp procedures in the Cave.<BR><P>------------------<BR><B>BobC</B><BR><B>'85 2M6 Targa</B><BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/targsig.jpg"> <BR><B>Suncoast Fieros</B>||||reg||1
Z||000003||johnmac||10-11-2001||02:35 PM||||Has anyone 'pickled' the flushing water in their Fiero before flushing?<P>Like adding ammonia to the flushing water and let it set overnight?<P>Want to get rid of all the accumulation of crud in the system before I add the good stuff!<P>Any suggestions on this ideal?<BR><P>------------------<BR>John MacHaffie<BR>86GT "Yellowbird"<BR>12+ month project car<BR>johnmac@mpinet.net<BR>Winter Springs, Florida||||reg||1
Z||000004||jfuredy||10-11-2001||06:43 PM||||Prestone makes a couple of different types of coolant system cleaners.  One is more for rust and scale, and the other is more for sludge, or at least that was my impression.  I would use one of these products to clean your system.  (I used the Super Cleaner to get rid of some serious scale in my system.)  I would stay away from harsher chemicals like ammonia or acids because they can damage the metal in your system.<P>Good luck with it,<BR>Joe Furedy<BR>'88 Formula<BR>Phoenix, AZ||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||10-11-2001||06:53 PM||||Use a comercial flushing prduct but Do Not use the acid flushes that are available. (Acid flushes are only for aluminum free systems. They are usually a 2 part process)<P>Main thing is drain everything you can first. Flush with clear water.<P>If you have allot of oil in the system then you might want to try some Simple Green or CitriSolv type cleaner in it. These will put the oil into suspension and rise clean easily. Fill the system carfully to avoid foaming the cleaner and eliminate all the air you can from the system as well. Then run it a few minutes to circulate and WARM everything. Then CAREFULLY vent the system pressure and drain the system while it's still warm. You only want the water to warm up. Don't run it to full operating temp.<P>There's a bunch of stuff on this in the engine section of my cave.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||extinct||10-11-2001||07:21 PM||||About those drain plugs in the cooling tubes.<BR>How do you get them out.  They are frozen/rusted on so tight, that I was afraid I would strip/bend the surrounding metal. Anyone else have this problem??||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fallman||10-23-2001||02:23 PM||||bump||||reg||1
Z||000008||88CoupeV6||10-23-2001||03:10 PM||||I have to flush the both of mine tomorrow.<P>------------------<BR>Josh<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/newsig.jpg">    <BR>:D  <BR><A HREF="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumList?u=1249362" TARGET=_blank>My pics</A>||||reg||9
Z||000009||theogre||10-23-2001||07:56 PM||||If you can't get the plugs out.... disconnect the rubber lines and slide some plastic tubing down there and suck it out with something. <P>They make a fluid sucker that looks kind of like the magazine of a grease gun. That would work.<P>you can also get one of those pumps you run with an electric drill. (Self piming and will work with cord or cordless drills) Those often come with suction tubing long enough for this job.<P>You want to get out all the old coolant you can. The stuff will kill pretty much anything living. You want to put as little as posible on the ground. Don't dump it in septic systems or cess pools either. ||||reg||1
